# docs/ splits by durability, not by directory-wide rule.
# Tracked: docs/changelog/ (the human narrative), docs/delivered.jsonl (the index) and
# docs/deliveries/ (the working documents of a closed-out plan, archived by /add-framework--done).
# Local: the working artefacts below — a fresh clone is not meant to carry them.
docs/plans/
docs/brainstorming/
docs/evidence/
.agents/*
.pff/*
.pff-builder/*
nul
tmpclaude*
.worktrees/

# Generated by `node scripts/build.js` from mcp/, and packed into the npm
# tarball by cli/package.json's `files` whitelist. Tracking it would put two
# copies of one source under review; the release workflow builds before it
# publishes, so CI always has it.
cli/src/mcp/

# Per-agent memory, written by a dispatched subagent while it works. It is
# learning about THIS checkout — what a reviewer confirmed, what it should stop
# flagging — and it accumulates per machine rather than per delivery. Tracking
# it would put one agent's notes into another's review diff, under a commit
# message that does not describe them.
.claude/agent-memory/

# Vitest's own cache, created under cli/ by any `npx vitest run`. Not
# configured anywhere — it is the runner's default — and it is per machine, so
# it belongs to nobody's diff.
cli/.vitest/

# The docs-corpus cache the MCP writes when it runs at the repository root.
# It is build output, and `delivered.sh`'s corpus is "every file git does not
# ignore" — so leaving it untracked put a dump of every node's id, path and
# TL;DR into the anchor-verification haystack, which is the exact failure that
# rule's own header says it exists to prevent.
#
# ⛔ THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING. Written bare as `.codeadd/`, this matched
#    a directory of that name at ANY depth — including `framwork/.codeadd/`, the
#    product layer's entire source of truth. The 216 files already tracked there
#    kept working, because .gitignore does not reach a tracked file, so nothing
#    broke and CI stayed green; what broke was every file added AFTERWARDS. A
#    new command, skill or reference was invisible to `git add` and to
#    `git status`, with no error anywhere. Anchor it to the root.
/.codeadd/

# Workbench build output — `node scripts/build-workbench.js` compiles
# workbench/{commands,skills,agents}/ into these. The SOURCE is workbench/,
# which is tracked; what lands here is generated and is not.
#
# ⛔ THE THREE SUBDIRECTORIES, NEVER THE PROVIDER DIRECTORY ITSELF.
#    `.claude/settings.json` is authored by hand and must stay tracked, and
#    `.claude/agent-memory/` already has its own rule above. Written bare as
#    `.claude/`, this would untrack the settings file and nobody would notice
#    until a fresh clone came up without it.
#
# ⛔ THE LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING, for the reason the /.codeadd/ comment
#    above records: written bare, each line would match a directory of that
#    name at ANY depth — including workbench/skills/*/ — and the source tree
#    would silently stop being added.
/.claude/commands/
/.claude/skills/
/.claude/agents/
/.opencode/commands/
/.opencode/skills/
/.opencode/agents/
